{
  "refuted": ["H3", "H6"],
  "diagnosis": "Probe foot_x1_5 (increasing foot length 1.5x) improved mean fitness by +0.41 but did not yield any walking rollouts, supporting H2/H5 (foot too short relative to leg) while showing that foot length alone is insufficient for walking. Probe legs_x0_8 (scaling legs to 0.8x) decreased mean fitness by -0.23 and also produced zero walking, contradicting H3/H6 which predicted that shortening legs would raise the natural pendulum frequency and improve the controller frequency match. The negative result may be due to overshooting the reference leg length (0.8×0.6707 ≈ 0.537 < 0.6) and/or the controller not being retuned. Hypotheses H1 and H4 (controller frequency/gain mismatch due to longer legs and higher mass) remain untested and are now the leading candidates. The baseline controller frequency ratio (1.304) and step frequency (2.11 Hz) suggest a timing mismatch. Competing hypotheses suggest stride amplitudes (hip_amp, knee_amp) may be too small (H1/H4 competing) or foot placement feedback (fp_kd, fp_kv) could compensate for short foot (H2/H5 competing). Training runs (plans r1p0/r1p1) have achieved walking counts of 8 with mean fitness ~5, indicating the controller can be tuned for this morphology; probes have not yet found walking morphologies.",
